SUMMARY: Journal of Audiovisual Ethnomusicology Shorts Program | Addition
 al Films and Guests
DESCRIPTION:This screening is a public rental presented by the Journal fo
 r Audiovisual Ethnomusicology (JAVEM)\, a peer-reviewed journal dedicated
  to audiovisual scholarship in ethnomusicology and related fields. Togeth
 er\, the three films demonstrate the range of audiovisual ethnomusicology
  across generations: from Hugo Zemp’s pioneering use of film as a tool fo
 r musical analysis\, to Richard K. Wolf’s longitudinal and collaborative 
 engagement with Kota musical and ritual life\, to Matthew Machin-Autenrie
 th and Dario Ranocchiari’s exploration of music\, history\, and cultural 
 memory in contemporary Granada.\n\nThe Song of Harmonics/Le chant des har
 moniques (dir. Hugo Zemp\, 1989\, France): Made with ethnomusicologist an
 d musician Trần Quang Hải\, Hugo Zemp’s landmark film investigates overto
 ne\, or biphonic\, singing\, a technique in which a single singer produce
 s a low fundamental while selectively emphasizing its harmonics to create
  a second audible melody. Moving between instruction\, Mongolian khöömii 
 performance\, and scientific investigation\, the film uses spectral and p
 hysiological imaging to make otherwise invisible aspects of vocal product
 ion visible. The Song of Harmonics is also an important example of ethnom
 usicological filmmaking in which audiovisual media function not simply to
  document musical practice\, but as a method of research and analysis. Th
 is screening is presented in tribute to Hugo Zemp (1937–2026)\, a foundat
 ional figure in visual ethnomusicology\, who passed away on January 3\, 2
 026. [38 min\; documentary\; English and Mongolian]\n\nResonant Histories
 : Echoes of Al-Andalus in Granada (dir. Matthew Machin-Autenrieth and Dar
 io Ranocchiari\, 2026\, Spain): Resonant Histories traces the complex rel
 ationship between Arab-Andalusian music and flamenco in Granada\, a city 
 where encounters between North African and Spanish musicians have long sh
 aped musical life. Traveling through the streets of Sacromonte\, closely 
 associated with flamenco\, and the Albaicín\, Granada’s historic Arab qua
 rter\, the filmmakers draw on musicians\, musicologists\, historians\, an
 d local enthusiasts to explore the ways the city’s past continues to reso
 nate in the present. The film moves from colonial exhibitions and early t
 heatrical experiments combining Andalusí music and flamenco to Granada’s 
 contemporary music scene\, asking how histories of encounter\, exchange\,
  and representation are remembered—and continually remade—through musical
  practice. [59 min\; documentary\; Spanish with English subtitles]\n\nPot
 s of Millet\, Faces of Gold: Transformation (dir. Richard K. Wolf\, 2025\
 , India and USA): The first in a series of experimental ethnographic film
 s drawing on Richard K. Wolf’s 35 years of research with the Kota people 
 of South India’s Nilgiri Hills\, Transformation explores a community of f
 ewer than 2\,500 people whose history has included disease\, forced migra
 tion\, and disruptions in the transmission of traditional knowledge. The 
 film’s title evokes two poles of Kota ritual life: millet\, central to mu
 ltiday mortuary ceremonies\, and gold-colored offerings fashioned as face
 s on the ancient pillars of Kota temples. Conceived as a time capsule add
 ressed to Kota people 100 years in the future and narrated in Kota by Wol
 f’s longtime collaborator Duryodana\, the film explores music alongside t
 ransformations of materials\, bodies\, and spiritual entities. It moves f
 rom pottery and musical-instrument making to their roles in mortuary ritu
 al\, combining Wolf’s footage from across decades with David G. Mandelbau
 m’s archival film and cylinder recordings from 1938 to create a layered p
 ortrait of Kota village life\, historical change\, and ethnographic encou
 nter. [81 min\; documentary\; Kota and Tamil with English subtitles]
CATEGORIES:Free
